          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Source describes practical patterns; single source provides specific detail but lacks corroboration.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via **reciprocal rank fusion**, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Performance and Use Cases\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nThis makes it particularly effective in production RAG systems where queries span both structured, exact-match data and open-ended semantic intent.\\n\\n### Status\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its practical advantages over single-method approaches.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nA robust mechanism for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrieval methods. See hybrid retrieval above for its application in combining lexical and semantic search.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval overview: combines sparse and dense search with reciprocal rank fusion for robust multi-method retrieval in RAG systems.\\n\u0022}"
